  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Patchy fog this morning as high pressure builds\nover the area.\n\nThe former nor'easter, now weak low pressure, over the Gulf of Maine \ncontinues to depart the Northeast. High pressure centered near \nWest Virginia will build east today and into Wednesday. \n\nWith winds becoming nearly calm and abundant low level moisture\nover the area, patchy fog will develop during the early morning\nhours. Fog will lift and dissipate this morning, giving way to \nmostly sunny skies. Mild with slightly above normal temperatures\non Wednesday with highs in the mid to upper 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Tidal flooding continues to recede in the back bays \nof New Jersey and Delaware.\n\nTidal levels continue to decline and recede as offshore flow \ncontinues to allow the back bays to drain. While some of the \nback bays of Barnegat Bay, Rehoboth Bay, Jefferson Creek and \nLittle Assawoman Bay may see some spotty minor tidal flooding\ncontinue through Wednesday, the bulk of the threat has now\nended. As a result, no coastal flooding headlines are currently\nin effect.\n\nOutside of these areas, a few locales may touch spotty minor \nwith tonight's high tide cycle, otherwise, no further tidal \nflooding is expected elsewhere.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Unsettled weather possible Friday through\nSunday.\n\nA cold front is forecast to move across the area late Friday into \nFriday night. As it moves into the area, there will be a chance of \nshowers. However, as the vorticity impulse aloft weakens, the \nshowers may dissipate for many areas as the move eastward.\nThe frontal boundary will then stall south and east of the\nregion with a surface high wedging in from the north. A stronger\nhigh to the north would force the frontal boundary to stall\nfurther south, allowing for a drier Saturday. A weaker high and\na frontal boundary that stalls further north, however, would\nincrease the chance for showers across the Delmarva and southern\nNew Jersey. An area of low pressure is forecast to develop \nalong the stalled front on Saturday then move north Sunday. \nShowers will increase Saturday night into Sunday as a couple of \nshort wave/vorticity impulses move into the area, along with the\nsurface low pressure.\n\nHigh pressure will then build in behind the departing low to\nkeep conditions dry early next week.",
